# PSD-gaming environment example.
# Local development: copy this file to .env.local, replace every placeholder, then restart `npm run dev`.
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# One variable per Firebase Web config field. Get every value from:
#   Firebase Console -> Project settings -> General -> Your apps -> Web app -> SDK setup and configuration -> Config
#
# Rules for the values:
#   - Paste the bare value only: NO quotes, no trailing comma, no trailing semicolon.
#       right:  VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=my-arcade
#       wrong:  VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID="my-arcade",
#   - On Vercel: Project -> Settings -> Environment Variables. Add each VITE_FIREBASE_* name below with its
#     own value, enable it for Production AND Preview, then REDEPLOY. Vite embeds VITE_* values at build time.
#
# This config is public browser configuration, not a secret: it ships inside the built site.
# NEVER put a service-account JSON, Admin SDK private key, or OAuth client secret in any VITE_* value.
# The build stops with an error if it detects one.

# Firebase "apiKey"
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=YOUR_FIREBASE_API_KEY
# Firebase "authDomain" (bare host, no https://)
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-project.firebaseapp.com
# Firebase "projectId"
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project
# Firebase "storageBucket" (optional)
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-project.firebasestorage.app
# Firebase "messagingSenderId" (optional)
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=YOUR_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID
# Firebase "appId"
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=YOUR_FIREBASE_APP_ID
# Firebase "measurementId" (optional; this app does not use Analytics, so you can leave it out)
# VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# Optional, build-time only (deliberately NOT a VITE_ variable, so it is never sent to the browser):
# make the build fail instead of just warning when the Firebase config is missing or invalid.
# Useful for the Production environment on Vercel.
# REQUIRE_FIREBASE_CONFIG=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development only: talk to the Firebase Emulator Suite instead of the
# real project (npm run dev:emu / npm run emulators; needs Java 11+).
# This flag is IGNORED by `npm run build`, so a deployment can never point at
# localhost. The VITE_FIREBASE_* values above are still required: the emulator
# is only a different address for the same SDK.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_USE_FIREBASE_EMULATOR=1
# VITE_FIREBASE_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1
# VITE_FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_PORT=8080
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_PORT=9099

# Older alternative (still supported): the whole config as ONE line of raw JSON. It is only used when none of
# the VITE_FIREBASE_* variables above has a value, so remove or comment out the placeholders above if you use it.
# VITE_FIREBASE_CONFIG={"apiKey":"...","authDomain":"...","projectId":"...","storageBucket":"...","messagingSenderId":"...","appId":"..."}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY — for the Vercel serverless backend in api/ (NOT VITE_*, so
# these never reach the browser). The online backend used to be callable Cloud
# Functions; it now runs as Vercel functions under /api/*, which verify the
# caller's Firebase ID token and write through the Admin SDK.
#
# Add these in Vercel -> Project -> Settings -> Environment Variables (Production
# AND Preview). They are only read by the api/ functions at request time, so a
# local `npm run dev` does not need them (the browser's online actions simply
# reach the deployed api/ if you point at it, or fail with a clear message).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Admin SDK credentials for the api/ functions. On Vercel there are no Application
# Default Credentials, so the backend needs a service account. Paste the service
# account JSON as ONE line (Firebase Console -> Project settings -> Service accounts
# -> Generate new private key -> download the JSON, then minify it onto one line).
# This is a SECRET: never prefix it with VITE_ and never commit it. Without it the
# functions fall back to default credentials, which only work on Google Cloud.
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=<the whole service-account JSON, minified onto one line>

# KEYLESS alternative — Workload Identity Federation (no downloadable key). Use this
# instead of FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT when an org policy blocks service-account key
# creation (constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation). Vercel signs a short-lived
# OIDC token; Google exchanges it and the functions impersonate a service account without
# ever holding a private key. Full setup (GCP pool/provider/IAM + these vars) is in
# docs/online-play.md -> "Keyless deploy". If FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT is also set, it wins.
# FIREBASE_WIF_AUDIENCE=<provider resource name, copied verbatim from `gcloud ... providers describe`>
#   e.g. //iam.googleapis.com/projects/<NUM>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/<POOL>/providers/<PROVIDER>
# FIREBASE_WIF_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=<service-account email to impersonate, e.g. psd-admin@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com>
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project
# Optional: comma-separated OAuth scopes (defaults to cloud-platform + Firestore + Auth).
# FIREBASE_WIF_SCOPES=https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform

# Optional: a shared secret that locks the scheduled cleanup route (api/cleanup).
# When set, /api/cleanup only runs for callers that send it as `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`
# or `?secret=<CRON_SECRET>`. Vercel Cron (see vercel.json `crons`) calls /api/cleanup without a
# header, so leave this UNSET to use the built-in Vercel Cron, or set it and drive cleanup from an
# external scheduler that can attach the header. The cleanup is idempotent either way.
# CRON_SECRET=choose-a-long-random-string

